nodecode-startup is a rewrite of Yicheng-Mark's Yicheng-Mark/hermes-windows-plugins:

  https://github.com/Yicheng-Mark/hermes-windows-plugins
  commit a2162423aeaa5f5b1379a9290de810ea48f9d12e
  MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 Yicheng-Mark (windows-startup/LICENSE)

What was rewritten: the question the plugin answers and the shape of its
answer. On Windows the same six Run/RunOnce keys are read (HKCU, HKLM and the
32-bit view), the same Explorer StartupApproved blobs are decoded into an
enabled/disabled verdict with a FILETIME of when the person last changed it
(state bytes 2 and 3 on, 6 and 7 off, anything else reported as unknown with
the raw byte), the same two Startup folders are listed through Explorer's User
Shell Folders so a redirected folder is found where the person has it, and the
same leftover approval records are reported separately rather than matched to
an entry that no longer exists. The command resolution is the original's:
quoting, arguments and environment variables folded in, the longest whitespace
prefix that names a real file for an unquoted command, a binary that is gone
said so, a relative path reported unchecked. The two tools, the filters, the
notes and the detail's fields keep the original's meaning; the tool names drop
the win_ prefix.

What was added: the same question on Linux and macOS, since a cell can run on
any of the three. Linux reads the XDG autostart folders, with a user's file
shadowing a system one of the same name as the desktop itself does, and counts
Hidden=true or X-GNOME-Autostart-enabled=false as switched off. macOS reads
the user's LaunchAgents and the system's LaunchAgents and LaunchDaemons, and
counts Disabled as switched off.

What was left out, and why: the plugin's Hermes tool registration, its pytest
suite and its VALIDATION.md, which are the harness's rather than the cell's,
and Explorer's `StartupApproved\Run32` handling beyond the keys above. The
registry is read through `reg query` rather than Python's winreg, because the
image has no registry reader: the parsing is the cell's own and is tested
against recorded output. A macOS launchd job stored as a binary plist is
reported by its file name with a note rather than decoded, and systemd user
units are not read.
